{% extends 'legal/_layout.html.twig' %}

{% block title %}Terms · {{ company.trading_name }}{% endblock %}
{% block meta_description %}Terms of use for the Kaltra service.{% endblock %}

{% block doc_title %}Terms{% endblock %}
{% block doc_subtitle %}
    Version {{ version }}. The Czech text is the binding one; this is a
    translation for convenience.
{% endblock %}

{% block doc_body %}
    <h2>1. Who provides the service</h2>
    <p>
        {{ company.trading_name }} is operated by <strong>{{ company.legal_name }}</strong>,
        company no. {{ company.company_number }}, registered at {{ company.street }},
        {{ company.postcode }} {{ company.city }}, Czech Republic ("the provider").
        Contact: <a href="mailto:{{ company.email }}">{{ company.email }}</a>,
        {{ company.phone }}.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>The provider is not registered for VAT.</strong> Prices shown are final.
    </p>

    <h2>2. Who the service is for</h2>
    <p>
        The service is for businesses only — medical practices and healthcare
        providers, acting in the course of their business. <strong>This is not a
        consumer contract</strong> and consumer contract provisions do not apply.
    </p>

    <h2>3. What the service does</h2>
    <p>
        Kaltra processes a patient export from the practice's software, works out
        whose preventive examination or dispensary review the health insurer will now
        pay for, and — once the practice has approved the list — sends those patients
        an SMS invitation in the practice's name.
    </p>
    <p>
        Which kinds of invitation a plan covers is set out on the
        <a href="{{ path('app_pricing') }}">pricing page</a>: preventive
        examinations are in every plan, dispensary reviews from the Group practice
        plan upwards. A practice on a lower plan still sees its chronic patients
        in the list and has them counted, but cannot send them an invitation.
    </p>

    <h3>What it does not do</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>It is not a booking system. Patients book directly with the practice.</li>
        <li>It does not connect into practice software. It works from an export the practice produces.</li>
        <li>It does not replace patient records or medical documentation.</li>
        <li>It gives no medical advice and makes no treatment decisions.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>4. Nothing sends without approval</h2>
    <p>
        No message goes out until the practice has approved both the list of
        recipients and the text. The system never sends on its own. Approval is the
        practice's decision and the practice is responsible for it.
    </p>

    <h2>5. Responsibility for data</h2>
    <p>
        The practice is responsible for the accuracy of the data it uploads. The
        provider calculates due dates from what the export contains; incorrect or
        out-of-date data produces an incorrect result.
    </p>
    <p>
        In particular, the practice is responsible for ensuring that the column it
        identifies as the date of the last preventive examination genuinely holds a
        preventive examination, and not any visit.
    </p>

    <h2>6. Insurer intervals</h2>
    <p>
        The intervals after which an insurer will pay for a procedure are set by the
        insurers and can change. The provider maintains them to the best of its
        knowledge but <strong>does not guarantee that any particular procedure will be
        reimbursed</strong>. Reimbursement is decided by the insurer, not the provider.
    </p>

    <h2>7. Prices and payment</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>Plan prices are on the <a href="{{ path('app_pricing') }}">pricing page</a>. Plans differ in the number of registered patients, the SMS bundle included, and the kinds of invitation covered.</li>
        <li>The patient count given for a plan is indicative: exceeding it does not stop sending, but the provider may ask the practice to move to the appropriate plan.</li>
        <li><strong>The subscription is paid in advance</strong> for the period ahead, charged to the card on the day the practice activates a plan and at the start of each period thereafter.</li>
        <li><strong>SMS beyond the bundle are billed in arrears</strong> for the period just ended — they cannot be counted before it. They appear on the following invoice.</li>
        <li>Charging is per SMS, not per invitation: a message with Czech diacritics fits 70 characters per SMS instead of 160, so a longer text is usually two. The count is shown while writing.</li>
        <li>Only messages actually sent are counted. A message that failed to send counts neither against the bundle nor as overage.</li>
        <li><strong>A change of plan takes effect immediately.</strong> The new plan starts a new billing period, charged that day; the unused remainder of the previous period is neither refunded nor carried over.</li>
        <li>Invoices are emailed as PDF. Payment terms are 14 days; where the amount is charged to the card, the invoice also serves as the receipt.</li>
        <li>If a charge fails, sending does not stop — patients are already expecting the invitation. The amount remains due and the provider will ask the practice to settle it.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>8. Referral discount</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>Where another practice registers through a practice's referral link and pays its first invoice, the referring practice's monthly fee is reduced by <strong>15%</strong>.</li>
        <li>The reduction applies to at most <strong>twelve invoices</strong> per referral, and only <strong>while the referred practice remains subscribed</strong>. If the referred practice cancels, that reduction ends with the next invoice; if it resubscribes, the remaining months continue.</li>
        <li>Reductions for several referrals add together, up to a maximum of <strong>45%</strong> of the fee.</li>
        <li>The reduction applies to the monthly fee only, not to SMS beyond the bundle.</li>
        <li>A reduction takes effect on the next invoice issued; invoices already issued are not adjusted retrospectively.</li>
        <li>A referral cannot be claimed between practices sharing a company number. The provider may withdraw a reduction where the referred practice's first payment is refunded or charged back, or where a referral is plainly contrived.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>9. Term and termination</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>The subscription is monthly, with no commitment.</li>
        <li>The practice may cancel at any time in its account. It can keep sending until the end of the period already paid for; after that, sending stops.</li>
        <li>A period paid for but not used is not refunded — the practice keeps the service until it ends.</li>
        <li>Once the last paid period ends we issue a final invoice for any SMS sent beyond the bundle in that period. It carries no monthly fee, and where there is nothing to bill it is not issued at all.</li>
        <li>The provider may terminate on 30 days' notice, or without notice on a material breach or misuse of the service.</li>
        <li>After termination, patient data is deleted on request. Invoices and accounting records are retained as the law requires.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>10. Availability</h2>
    <p>
        The provider aims for continuous availability but does not guarantee it. SMS
        are sent Monday to Friday between 08:00 and 18:00 (Europe/Prague); messages
        queued outside those hours go out in the next window. Delivery depends on the
        mobile operator and cannot be guaranteed by the provider.
    </p>

    <h2>11. Limitation of liability</h2>
    <p>
        The provider is liable for damage caused by breach of these terms. Total
        liability is limited to the amount the practice paid in the 12 months before
        the damage arose.
    </p>
    <p>
        The provider is not liable in particular for an insurer declining to reimburse
        a procedure, for non-delivery of an SMS by an operator, for the consequences
        of incorrect data in an uploaded export, or for lost profit. This limitation
        does not apply to damage caused intentionally or by gross negligence, nor to
        harm to natural rights of a person.
    </p>

    <h2>12. Data protection</h2>
    <p>
        The practice is the controller of patient data; the provider is the processor.
        Details are in the <a href="{{ path('app_privacy') }}">privacy policy</a> and
        in the processing agreement the practice accepts before uploading anything.
    </p>

    <h2>13. Changes to these terms</h2>
    <p>
        The provider may change these terms. Material changes are announced by email
        at least 30 days in advance. A practice that does not accept a change may
        cancel before it takes effect.
    </p>

    <h2>14. Governing law</h2>
    <p>
        This relationship is governed by the law of the Czech Republic. Disputes are
        decided by the Czech courts having jurisdiction over the provider's
        registered office.
    </p>
{% endblock %}

